    "K20": {
        "V0": "A hybrid of light opera and musical theatre which was popular from the late 1800s thru the early-to-mid 1900s.",
        "V1": "A hybrid of light opera and musical theatre which was popular from the late 1800s thru the early-to-mid 1900s."
    },
    "K21": {
        "V0": "Usually the music was written in a musical theatre vein with heavy operatic flavoring, and the shows had book scenes like traditional musicals as opposed to operatic recitatives.\nFamous operetta composers include Gilbert & Sullivan, Victor Herbert, Sigmund Romberg, Wright & Forrest and Rudolf Friml.\n\nhttp://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operetta\n\nhttp://www.musicaltheatreguide.com/mainmenu.htm\n\nReleases that should be tagged as operetta:\nThe Desert Song / The New Moon / The Blue Train [Original London Casts]\nThe Pirates of Penzance \nThe Mikado\nBabes in Toyland [Radio Broadcast - 1949]\nSong of Norway [Studio Cast]\nKismet [Original Broadway Cast]\nand many, many more...",
        "V1": "Show tunes are songs from musicals, like Rodgers and Hammerstein et al.  I don't really think this form of light opera belongs there, next to West Side Story, Cabaret, and Oklahoma. :/\n\nDoes this really sound like a Show Tune?\n\nhttp://ca.youtube.com/watch?v=4YBhKx1bkEM\n\nhttp://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Show_tunes\n\nAnd I wouldn't feel comfortable categorizing Operetta as Soundtracks any more than I would Opera."
    },
